@startuml
' Source of truth for: assets/diagrams/learning/data-plane/ngfw/vector-processing.svg
' Re-render with: plantuml -tsvg vector-processing.puml
title Why "Vector" Packet Processing\n(a node processes a whole batch at once)

skinparam shadowing false
skinparam rectangleRoundCorner 8
skinparam defaultTextAlignment center

rectangle "Input frame (a vector)\n[pkt0][pkt1][pkt2]...[pkt N-1]\nup to 256 packet indices" as IN #eff6ff

package "One VPP node function" {
  rectangle "Dual loop — 2 packets at a time\nprefetch next buffers while processing\nthe current pair -> hides memory latency" as DUAL #ffffff
  rectangle "Single loop — leftover packets\nhandles the remainder one at a time" as SINGLE #ffffff
}

rectangle "-> snort-ip4-input\n(packets to inspect)" as O1 #ecfdf5
rectangle "-> ip4-lookup\n(passed packets)" as O2 #eff6ff
rectangle "-> esp4-encrypt\n(packets to encrypt)" as O3 #fff7ed

IN --> DUAL
DUAL --> SINGLE
SINGLE --> O1
SINGLE --> O2
SINGLE --> O3

legend bottom
Processing a batch keeps the node's code and data hot in CPU cache, so per-packet
overhead is amortized to a few nanoseconds. This is why VPP hits line rate in software.
endlegend
@enduml
